Below are the columns that will matter for building the weather‑summary query, followed by ten exploratory Snowflake SQL snippets that move from very simple inspection to almost the full logic that the final report will need.

Relevant columns and their potential use
• "creation_time": filter to forecasts CREATED in July‑2019.  
• "geography": grid‑point (GEOGRAPHY); use ST_DISTANCE to keep only points ≤ 5 000 m from POINT(51.5 26.75).  
• "geography_polygon": optional; can also be used with a spatial test instead of the point.  
• "forecast": VARIANT array; flatten to pull elements such as  
  – "hours" (forecast lead time)  
  – "temperature_2m_above_ground" (°C)  
  – "total_precipitation_surface" or similar precipitation keys (inspect first)  
  – "cloud_cover" or similar cloud keys (inspect first)  
• (No other tables are needed; everything comes from NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM.NOAA_GFS0P25.)

Sample investigation queries
Every query returns at most 20 rows and every identifier is enclosed in double‑quotes.

```sql
-- 1. Quick peek at creation times that fall in July‑2019
SELECT DISTINCT "creation_time"
FROM "NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM"."NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM"."NOAA_GFS0P25"
WHERE "creation_time" BETWEEN 1561939200000000   -- 2019‑07‑01 00:00:00 UTC (µs)
                          AND 1564617599000000   -- 2019‑07‑31 23:59:59 UTC (µs)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Visualise the GEOGRAPHY point (as text) together with creation time
SELECT ST_ASTEXT("geography")          AS "wkt_point",
       "creation_time"
FROM   "NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM"."NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM"."NOAA_GFS0P25"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. All grid points that lie inside a 5‑km radius of 26.75 N, 51.5 E
SELECT ST_ASTEXT("geography") AS "grid_point",
       ST_DISTANCE(
           "geography",
           TO_GEOGRAPHY('POINT(51.5 26.75)')
       )                      AS "distance_m"
FROM   "NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM"."NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM"."NOAA_GFS0P25"
WHERE  ST_DISTANCE(
           "geography",
           TO_GEOGRAPHY('POINT(51.5 26.75)')
       ) <= 5000
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Flatten one example row to see the JSON keys contained in "forecast"
SELECT  f.value AS "forecast_element"
FROM   "NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM"."NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM"."NOAA_GFS0P25",
       LATERAL FLATTEN( input => "forecast" ) f
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Inspect temperatures and lead‑times at a single grid point & creation time
SELECT  f.value:"hours"::INT                                    AS "lead_hr",
        f.value:"temperature_2m_above_ground"::FLOAT           AS "temp_c"
FROM    "NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM"."NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM"."NOAA_GFS0P25",
        LATERAL FLATTEN( input => "forecast" ) f
WHERE   ST_DISTANCE(
           "geography",
           TO_GEOGRAPHY('POINT(51.5 26.75)')
        ) <= 5000
  AND   "creation_time" BETWEEN 1561939200000000
                          AND 1564617599000000
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Same as (5) but keep only forecasts that are for the “next day” (24‑47 h)
SELECT  f.value:"hours"::INT                      AS "lead_hr",
        f.value:"temperature_2m_above_ground"::FLOAT AS "temp_c",
        "creation_time"
FROM    "NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM"."NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM"."NOAA_GFS0P25",
        LATERAL FLATTEN( input => "forecast" ) f
WHERE   ST_DISTANCE("geography", TO_GEOGRAPHY('POINT(51.5 26.75)')) <= 5000
  AND   "creation_time" BETWEEN 1561939200000000 AND 1564617599000000
  AND   f.value:"hours"::INT BETWEEN 24 AND 47
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Check what precipitation‑related keys exist in the variant
SELECT DISTINCT OBJECT_KEYS(f.value) AS "precip_keys"
FROM   "NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM"."NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM"."NOAA_GFS0P25",
       LATERAL FLATTEN( input => "forecast" ) f
WHERE  ST_DISTANCE("geography", TO_GEOGRAPHY('POINT(51.5 26.75)')) <= 5000
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Pull precipitation, cloud cover and temperature for next‑day hours
SELECT  f.value:"hours"::INT                                   AS "lead_hr",
        f.value:"temperature_2m_above_ground"::FLOAT          AS "temp_c",
        f.value:"total_precipitation_surface"::FLOAT          AS "tot_precip_mm",
        f.value:"cloud_cover"::FLOAT                          AS "cloud_cover_pct",
        "creation_time"
FROM    "NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM"."NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM"."NOAA_GFS0P25",
        LATERAL FLATTEN( input => "forecast" ) f
WHERE   ST_DISTANCE("geography", TO_GEOGRAPHY('POINT(51.5 26.75)')) <= 5000
  AND   "creation_time" BETWEEN 1561939200000000 AND 1564617599000000
  AND   f.value:"hours"::INT BETWEEN 24 AND 47
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Aggregate by forecast DATE (the valid‑time) for temperatures and precip
SELECT  DATE_FROM_PARTS(1970,1,1)
          + (f.value:"time"::NUMBER / 1000000)::INT / 86400  AS "valid_date",
        MAX( f.value:"temperature_2m_above_ground"::FLOAT )  AS "tmax_c",
        MIN( f.value:"temperature_2m_above_ground"::FLOAT )  AS "tmin_c",
        AVG( f.value:"temperature_2m_above_ground"::FLOAT )  AS "tavg_c",
        SUM( f.value:"total_precipitation_surface"::FLOAT )  AS "tot_precip_mm"
FROM   "NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM"."NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM"."NOAA_GFS0P25",
       LATERAL FLATTEN( input => "forecast" ) f
WHERE  ST_DISTANCE("geography", TO_GEOGRAPHY('POINT(51.5 26.75)')) <= 5000
  AND  "creation_time" BETWEEN 1561939200000000 AND 1564617599000000
  AND  f.value:"hours"::INT BETWEEN 24 AND 47
GROUP  BY 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Sketch of the almost‑final daily summary including cloud cover & split rain/snow
SELECT
  DATE_FROM_PARTS(1970,1,1)
    + (f.value:"time"::NUMBER / 1000000)::INT / 86400       AS "valid_date",

  MAX(f.value:"temperature_2m_above_ground"::FLOAT)         AS "tmax_c",
  MIN(f.value:"temperature_2m_above_ground"::FLOAT)         AS "tmin_c",
  AVG(f.value:"temperature_2m_above_ground"::FLOAT)         AS "tavg_c",

  AVG(CASE 
        WHEN f.value:"hours"::INT BETWEEN 26 AND 33 -- ≈10‑17 local, adjust if needed
        THEN f.value:"cloud_cover"::FLOAT
      END)                                                 AS "cloud_cover_pct_10_17",

  SUM(f.value:"total_precipitation_surface"::FLOAT)         AS "tot_precip_mm",

  SUM( CASE 
          WHEN f.value:"temperature_2m_above_ground"::FLOAT < 0
          THEN f.value:"total_precipitation_surface"::FLOAT
       END )                                                AS "snow_mm",

  SUM( CASE 
          WHEN f.value:"temperature_2m_above_ground"::FLOAT >= 0
          THEN f.value:"total_precipitation_surface"::FLOAT
       END )                                                AS "rain_mm"

FROM  "NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM"."NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM"."NOAA_GFS0P25",
      LATERAL FLATTEN( input => "forecast" ) f
WHERE ST_DISTANCE("geography", TO_GEOGRAPHY('POINT(51.5 26.75)')) <= 5000
  AND "creation_time" BETWEEN 1561939200000000 AND 1564617599000000
  AND f.value:"hours"::INT BETWEEN 24 AND 47                 -- next‑day only
GROUP BY 1
LIMIT 20;
```

These ten queries should give you a progressive understanding of the data, the JSON keys inside the “forecast” column, and how to apply the spatial / temporal filters needed for the requested daily weather summary.
